; The built-in `Int` extern must be emitted when the ONLY references to it come from emitted type
; aliases. `generate_int` is gated on `is_referenced("Int")`, whose reference walk covers emitted
; type aliases (not just rust structs), so a rule that classifies as a plain `pub type` alias over an
; `int`-bearing base still registers the reference and gets its `Int` definition. Regression: the walk
; visited rust structs only, so an alias-only `int` reference emitted `pub type X = Int;` naming an
; `Int` no one minted (`cannot find type Int`, E0425) — a dangle in both the rust and wasm crates.
;
; The three rules span the descent the fix must cover: `bare_int` is a top-level `Rust(Int)` alias
; base; `cbor_int` nests it under a `.cbor` bytes wrapper; `cbor_int_table` nests it as a table VALUE
; under a `.cbor` bytes wrapper (`Map(_, Rust(Int))`). All three are UNCONSTRAINED — a control (e.g.
; `int .ne 1`) instead makes a RangeCheck wrapper STRUCT that the rust-struct walk already covered, so
; the alias classification is exactly the untested path. The control sibling lives in
; tagged_constrained_int.cddl (`cbor_ctl_ne = bytes .cbor (int .ne 1)`, with a holder).
;
; Deliberately holder-FREE: embedding any of these in a record member (`h = [f: bare_int]`) registers
; `Int` through the record's field walk, and `Int` is a crate-global extern emitted once for the whole
; crate — so a single holder would define `Int` for the whole fixture and MASK the alias-only dangle
; this fixture exists to pin (the same batch-masking that let the class hide behind int-referencing
; batch-mates in the recombination sweep). The round-trip/decode side is covered by the
; decode-conformance CORPUS leg, which holder-wraps each rule in its own synthetic probe
; (`__probe_holder = [0, <rule>]`): `bare_int` and `cbor_int` mint spec-derived vectors, while
; `cbor_int_table`'s inline-composite `.cbor` controller hits the ruby generator's parse gap and is
; pinned (the same class as cbor_bignint_table's `.cbor { … }` rule).
bare_int = int
cbor_int = bytes .cbor int
cbor_int_table = bytes .cbor { * tstr => int }
